rpus = 200 words sampled with seed 3 from pattern prefixes un/re/pre + stems play/read/code/train + suffixes ing/ed/er/s. That gives 3*4*4 = 48 combinations... need 200 words with repetition sampling. Sample 200 with seed 3.\n\nVocab building: BPE-like frequency procedure or character pairs. I\'ll do simple: start with characters, then iteratively merge most frequent adjacent pairs (BPE) up to some vocab size. Then tokenize uses greedy longest-prefix match with \'##\' continuation.\n\nDocumented rule for UNK fallback: if greedy match fails at any position (no vocab piece matches), the whole word becomes single [UNK].\n\nTests:\n- every corpus word tokenizes with zero [UNK]\n- \'unplayable\' where \'able\' missing from vocab yields single [UNK]\n- mean tokens-per-word <= 3.0\n- deterministic\n\nNeed to ensure vocab covers all corpus words fully so no [UNK]. With BPE merges learned on corpus, all corpus words decompose into pieces — but greedy matching could fail if merges produce non-prefix-friendly segmentation? Greedy longest-match from left; since all subwords in vocab include individual chars? Need to ensure single characters of each word are in vocab. If we keep initial alphabet (all chars), greedy will always find at least one char match, unless char has \'##\' prefix issue: continuation pieces need \'##\'+char in vocab. Include both plain chars and \'##\'chars in initial vocab to guarantee full coverage.\n\nMean tokens per word <= 3.0: with enough merges, fine. Words like "unplaying" length 9; need avg pieces <= 3 over corpus. BPE with vocab size like 100-200 should do it. I\'ll tune: run merges until average tokens/word target met or max merges.\n\nSimplest: learn merges greedily until number of merges e.g. 150 or stop when most frequent pair frequency < 2. Then compute stats and assert in test. Better make vocab building adaptive: keep merging while best pair count >= 2 and len(vocab) < max_vocab_size. Then check mean tokens <= 3.0 in test; choose parameters so it holds. Let me just implement and verify by running pytest.\n\nCorpus generation:\n\n```python\nimport random\n_PREFIXES = ["un", "re", "pre"]\n_STEMS = ["play", "read", "code", "train"]\n_SUFFIXES = ["ing", "ed", "er", "s"]\n\ndef build_corpus(n=200, seed=3):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    combos = [p+s+t for p in _PREFIXES for s in _STEMS for t in _SUFFIXES]\n    return rng.choices(combos, k=n)\n```\n\nWait "pattern (prefixes un/re/pre + stems play/read/code/train + suffixes ing/er/ed/s)" → 3*4*4=48 unique words, sample 200 with seed 3.\n\nVocab build (BPE):\n\n```python\ndef build_vocab(corpus, num_merges=..., special_tokens=("[UNK]",)):\n    word_freqs = Counter(corpus)\n    # splits: each word -> list of symbols; first char plain, rest \'##\'+char\n    ...\n```\n\nStandard BPE on pretokenized words with wordpiece-style continuation marks. Merge pairs, when merging inside word, merged piece representation: first symbol without ## if at start else with ##. Represent splits as list of strings already including ## markers; merging pair (a,b) -> a+b (concatenation keeps ## marker correct since b\'s ## is internal).\n\nAfter learning merges, vocab = set of all resulting symbols + specials + maybe base chars both forms. Actually initial symbols: first char as-is, others \'##\'+char. Vocab = union of all symbols ever seen plus final. Just collect vocab from final splits plus ensure all initial symbols included.\n\ntokenize(word):\n```python\ndef tokenize(word, vocab=None):\n    vocab = _VOCAB if vocab is None else vocab\n    if word in vocab... actually check whole word first? Greedy longest-match naturally handles that.\n    tokens = []\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(word):\n        matched = None\n        for j in range(len(word), i, -1):\n            piece = word[i:j] if i == 0 else "##" + word[i:j]\n            if piece in vocab:\n                matched = piece\n                end = j\n                break\n        if matched is None:\n            return ["[UNK]"]  # documented rule\n        tokens.append(matched)\n        i = end\n    return tokens\n```\n\nRule documented: any position with no matching piece → entire word maps to single [UNK].\n\n\'unplayable\': vocab built from corpus contains letters u,n,p,l,a,y,b,e etc.? \'b\' appears? Corpus stems: play, read, code, train; suffixes ing, ed, er, s. Letters present: u,n,r,e,p,l,a,y,d,c,o,t,i,g,s. No \'b\'. So \'##b\' not in vocab → \'unplayable\' fails at position of \'b\'? Greedy: u,n, then \'##p\',\'##pl\',... up through \'playable\'? pieces: after \'un\', try longest: \'##playable\'? not in vocab. ... eventually \'##p\' matched, then \'l\',\'a\',\'y\', then \'##a\',\'##ab\'? \'b\' alone: \'##b\' not in vocab, and no longer match containing b → fail → return [\'[UNK]\']. \n\nBut wait — greedy might match \'un\' as a piece? \'un\' in vocab yes. Fine either way, result still [UNK] overall because \'##ab...\' can\'t proceed past b. Actually careful: could greedy match consume up to exactly \'b\'-start such that remaining starts elsewhere? The failure occurs whenever we reach position of \'b\' regardless of path, since \'##b\' absent and any longer piece containing b also absent (vocab only has corpus letters). So single [UNK]. Good.\n\nMean tokens-per-word <= 3.0: need enough merges. Words are 6-9 chars ("presodings"? no—"pre"+stem+"ing" max = pre+train+ing = "pretraining" 11 chars). Average word ~8-9 chars. Need avg pieces ≤3, so pieces avg length ≥~3. BPE merges will handle common chunks like "ing", "##ing", "re", "un", "pre", "train". Let me pick num_merges generously (e.g., merge until best freq < 2, cap 300). Verify empirically via test run.\n\nDeterminism: pure function of vocab + input; test runs twice.\n\nAlso expose VOCAB built at module import from inline corpus, and functions build_corpus, build_vocab, tokenize.\n\nWrite files and run pytest.', 'thinkingSignature': 'reasoning'}, {'type': 'toolCall', 'id': '98f5db85-3463-4e8c-a39d-f13467a6d210', 'name': 'write', 'arguments': {'path': '/workspace/wordpiece.py', 'content': '"""Greedy longest-match WordPiece-style tokenizer with a programmatically\nlearned vocabulary.\n\nVocabulary learning\n-------------------\nThe vocabulary is derived from the inline corpus (below) using a BPE-like\niterative pair-merge procedure over WordPiece-style symbols: each word starts\nsplit into characters, where the first character is bare and every subsequent\ncharacter is prefixed with \'##\'. The most frequent adjacent symbol pair is\nmerged repeatedly (its concatenation becomes a new vocab entry) until no pair\noccurs at least twice or a merge cap is reached.\n\nTokenization rule\n-----------------\n``tokenize(word)`` scans left-to-right, always consuming the longest piece\nfound in the vocabulary (bare piece at position 0, \'##\'-prefixed piece\nelsewhere). Documented [UNK] rule: if at any position no vocabulary piece\nmatches, the ENTIRE word is emitted as a single \'[UNK]\' token.\n"""\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport random\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nUNK = "[UNK]"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Inline corpus: 200 words sampled with seed 3 from the pattern\n#   (prefix in {un, re, pre}) + (stem in {play, read, code, train})\n#   + (suffix in {ing, ed, er, s})\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n_PREFIXES = ("un", "re", "pre")\n_STEMS = ("play", "read", "code", "train")\n_SUFFIXES = ("ing", "ed", "er", "s")\n\n\ndef build_corpus(n: int = 200, seed: int = 3) -> list[str]:\n    """Sample n words (with replacement, seeded) from the morphological pattern."""\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    combos = [p + s + t for p in _PREFIXES for s in _STEMS for t in _SUFFIXES]\n    return rng.choices(combos, k=n)\n\n\nCORPUS = build_corpus()\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Vocabulary learning (BPE-like frequency procedure)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef _initial_split(word: str) -> list[str]:\n    """Word -> WordPiece-style char symbols (\'##\' on non-initial chars)."""\n    return [word[0]] + ["##" + ch for ch in word[1:]]\n\n\ndef build_vocab(\n    corpus: list[str],\n    min_freq: int = 2,\n    max_merges: int = 500,\n) -> tuple[set[str], list[tuple[str, str]]]:\n    """Learn a vocabulary from `corpus` via iterative most-frequent-pair merges.\n\n    Returns (vocab, merges). The vocab always contains every initial character\n    symbol (bare and \'##\' forms), all merged pieces, and the [UNK] token.\n    """\n    word_freqs = Counter(corpus)\n    splits = {w: _initial_split(w) for w in word_freqs}\n\n    vocab: set[str] = {UNK}\n    for w in splits:\n        vocab.update(splits[w])\n\n    merges: list[tuple[str, str]] = []\n\n    def pair_counts() -> Counter:\n        counts: Counter = Counter()\n        for w, freq in word_freqs.items():\n            syms = splits[w]\n            for a, b in zip(syms, syms[1:]):\n                counts[(a, b)] += freq\n        return counts\n\n    for _ in range(max_merges):\n        counts = pair_counts()\n        if not counts:\n            break\n        (best, freq) = max(counts.items(), key=lambda kv: (kv[1], kv[0]))\n        if freq < min_freq:\n            break\n        a, b = best\n        merged = a + b\n        merges.append(best)\n        vocab.add(merged)\n        for w, syms in splits.items():\n            i = 0\n            new_syms: list[str] = []\n            while i < len(syms):\n                if i < len(syms) - 1 and syms[i] == a and syms[i + 1] == b:\n                    new_syms.append(merged)\n                    i += 2\n                else:\n                    new_syms.append(syms[i])\n                    i += 1\n            splits[w] = new_syms\n\n    return vocab, merges\n\n\nVOCAB, MERGES = build_vocab(CORPUS)\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Tokenizer: greedy longest-prefix matching\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef tokenize(word: str, vocab: set[str] | None = None) -> list[str]:\n    """Greedy longest-match WordPiece tokenization of a single word.\n\n    At each position the longest matching vocabulary piece wins (\'##\'-prefixed\n    for non-initial positions). Documented rule: if no piece matches at some\n    position, the whole word is returned as a single [UNK].\n    """\n    v = VOCAB if vocab is None else vocab\n    tokens: list[str] = []\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(word):\n        match = None\n        for j in range(len(word), i, -1):  # longest first\n            piece = word[i:j] if i == 0 else "##" + word[i:j]\n            if piece in v:\n                match = piece\n                break\n        if match is None:\n            return [UNK]  # documented rule: single [UNK] for the whole word\n        tokens.append(match)\n        i = j\n    return tokens\n\n\ndef tokenize_corpus(corpus: list[str], vocab: set[str] | None = None) -> list[list[str]]:\n    return [tokenize(w, vocab) for w in corpus]\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    enc = tokenize_corpus(CORPUS)\n    print(f"vocab size: {len(VOCAB)}")\n    print(f"mean tokens/word: {sum(len(t) for t in enc) / len(enc):.3f}")\n    print(f"[UNK] count: {sum(\'[UNK]\' in t for t in enc)}")\n'}}, {'type': 'toolCall', 'id': '91e61e80-baa7-4dec-b40f-1875c970c421', 'name': 'write', 'arguments': {'path': '/workspace/test_wordpiece.py', 'content': 'import statistics\n\nfrom wordpiece import CORPUS, MERGES, UNK, VOCAB, build_corpus, tokenize\n\n\ndef test_corpus_shape():\n    corpus = build_corpus(n=200, seed=3)\n    assert corpus == CORPUS\n    assert len(CORPUS) == 200\n    allowed = {p + s + t for p in ("un", "re", "pre") for s in ("play", "read", "code", "train") for t in ("ing", "ed", "er", "s")}\n    assert set(CORPUS) <= allowed\n    assert len(MERGES) > 0